# Django + Tailwind Best Practices

- Use `base.html` as the global layout and make page templates extend it.
- Keep business logic in `views.py`/`forms.py`/`models.py`, not in templates.
- Put static assets in `static/` (`static/css`, `static/js`) and load them with `{% load static %}`.
- Avoid inline CSS/JS in templates; prefer reusable classes and external files.
- With Tailwind, prefer utility classes in templates; keep custom CSS minimal and focused.
- Use consistent spacing/color scale (`p-4`, `gap-4`, `text-slate-*`, `bg-blue-*`) across pages.
- Split repeated UI into reusable template partials/components.
- Keep templates readable: clear sections, semantic tags, short blocks.
- Use Django forms for validation and CSRF protection on all POST forms.
- For dashboard tables: include explicit empty state, readable headings, and responsive behavior.

## Tailwind Setup — CLI (never CDN)

**Never use the Tailwind CDN** (`cdn.tailwindcss.com`) in templates. All projects use Tailwind CLI v3.

- Source file : `static/css/input.css` (contains `@tailwind base/components/utilities` + `@layer components`)
- Output file : `static/css/app.css` (compiled, committed)
- Custom component classes (`.md-card`, `.md-btn-primary`, `.sidebar-link`, etc.) are defined in `input.css` under `@layer components` using `@apply`
- The theme (colors, fonts) is defined in `tailwind.config.js` at the repo root

```bash
npm run build:css   # build minifié (prod)
npm run watch:css   # rebuild automatique en dev
```

After adding new Tailwind classes to templates, always run `npm run build:css` so `app.css` stays up to date.

Load the compiled CSS in every template (including standalone auth pages):

```django
{% load static %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{% static 'css/app.css' %}">
```

## Minimal Template Pattern

```django
{% load static %}
{% extends "base.html" %}
{% block content %}
  <section class="p-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Page Title</h1>
  </section>
{% endblock %}
```
